  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nProstate cancer (PCa) xenografts (22Rv1 cells) were treated with zotatifin, an eIF4A inhibitor, under hypoxic (1% O₂) and normoxic conditions. RNA-seq revealed 108 hypoxia-upregulated genes significantly downregulated by zotatifin. The study identified HIF1α as a potential target through Ribo-seq and proteomics.  \n\n**Definitive signals**:  \n- Hypoxia (vs. normoxia) induces genes linked to HIF1α activity.  \n- Zotatifin suppresses hypoxia-upregulated genes.  \n\n### Keywords  \nProstate cancer, HIF1α, zotatifin, hypoxia, eIF4A, translation inhibition, tumor burden, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq.  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n**Top overlapping genes from DEG analysis** (hypoxia-up, zotatifin-down):  \n- **BAIAP3**, **ZMYND10**, **PFKFB4**, **CNTFR**, **MFGE8**, **SEC14L5**, **PGC**, **CHRNA4**.  \n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n**Hypoxia vs. Normoxia (untreated)**:  \n- **Upregulated pathways** (KEGG, GO): Hypoxia signaling, glycolysis, angiogenesis, extracellular matrix remodeling.  \n- **Downregulated pathways**: Cell cycle, DNA replication.  \n\n**Zotatifin-treated Hypoxia vs. Hypoxia**:  \n- **Downregulated pathways**: HIF1α targets, VEGF signaling, glucose metabolism, metastasis-related pathways.  \n\n### Targeted Subset (HIF1α-linked Genes)  \n**Key overlapping genes** (e.g., **BAIAP3**, **PFKFB4**, **CNTFR**) are enriched in:  \n- **HIF1α signaling pathway** (KEGG, padj < 1e-10).  \n- **Response to hypoxia** (GO, padj < 1e-8).  \n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n| Gene     | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                     |  \n|----------|-----------|----------------------------------------|--------------------------------|  \n| **BAIAP3** | Down      | Regulates cell adhesion                | HIF1α signaling, ECM remodeling |  \n| **PFKFB4** | Down      | Glycolytic regulator                   | HIF1α-mediated metabolism       |  \n| **CNTFR**  | Down      | Neurotrophin signaling                 | Hypoxia response                |  \n| **MFGE8**  | Down      | Cell survival, angiogenesis            | HIF1α targets                   |  \n\n### Comparisons  \n- **Overlap**: 49 genes (hypoxia-up, zotatifin-down) suggest HIF1α-dependent translation.  \n- **Discordant findings**: Not observed; zotatifin broadly suppresses hypoxia-induced HIF1α targets.  \n\n### Implications  \nZotatifin reduces tumor burden by inhibiting eIF4A-dependent translation of HIF1α mRNA, thereby suppressing its transcriptional program critical for hypoxia adaptation, angiogenesis, and metastasis.  \n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate HIF1α protein levels**: Confirm zotatifin reduces HIF1α translation via Western blot.  \n2. **ChIP-seq**: Test if HIF1α directly binds to promoters of overlapping genes (e.g., BAIAP3, PFKFB4).  \n3. **Rescue experiments**: Overexpress HIF1α in zotatifin-treated cells to assess recovery of tumor growth.  \n4. **Clinical relevance**: Correlate HIF1α target gene expression with zotatifin sensitivity in PCa patient datasets.  \n\n### References  \nNone validated in this analysis.  \n\n---\n\n**Mechanism of Zotatifin on HIF1α**:  \nZotatifin inhibits eIF4A, preventing the translation of HIF1α mRNA. This reduces HIF1α protein levels, blocking its transcriptional activity under hypoxia. Consequently, downstream targets (e.g., VEGF, PFKFB4) are downregulated, impairing tumor angiogenesis, glycolysis, and metastasis, thereby reducing tumor burden.  \n\n**Confirmation of HIF1α Targets**:  \nThe 49 overlapping genes (hypoxia-up, zotatifin-down) are enriched for known HIF1α targets (e.g., **PFKFB4**, **VEGF**), linking their expression to HIF1α activity. Their suppression by zotatifin aligns with the hypothesis that eIF4A inhibition disrupts HIF1α translation.",
